# Take an ESTABLISHED member out of the set and put it back (gpc-causes #173).
#
# The difference from mode-stby-run.script, and it looks like the whole
# difference: WAIT FOR THE TALKBACK FIRST.  In the session where votes
# appeared, GPC2's MODE talkback was reading RUN before the switch moved --
# GPC1 was hearing its ready discrete, so the two really were a set.  In the
# test that found nothing, the talkback was barberpole throughout, so nothing
# was taken out of anything.
#
# If a vote follows this, it is very likely CORRECT: a redundant set votes
# against a member that stops driving its lines, and FCMCSYNC re-admits one
# only on an observed 000 -> 111.  That would make #173 a misreading rather
# than a defect, and would explain the other symptom exactly -- OPS
# transitions stopping being done in common, because GPC2 had been voted out
# and never readmitted.
#
# A `wait` takes no time prefix and every other line needs one; times after
# a wait start again from when it returns.
#
# The second excursion is long on purpose: the session this reconstructs did
# it twice, the second time held much longer, because it was not clear the
# first had been seen -- and a POLL LOST on CRT2 was expected and did not
# come, which is its own question.
+0     gpc 2
wait gpc 2 mode-tb RUN timeout 300
+0     gpc 1
wait gpc 1 mode-tb RUN timeout 300
+10    gpc 2
+0     mode STANDBY
+15    mode RUN
